As we know, fork( ) creates a child process and duplicates the code of the parent process in the child process. There onwards the execution of the fork( ) function continues in both the processes. Thus the duplication code inside fork( ) is executed once, whereas the remaining code inside it is executed in both the parent as well as the child process. Hence control would come back from fork( ) twice, even though it is actually called only once. When control returns from fork( ) of the parent process it returns the PID of the child process, whereas when control returns from fork( ) of the child process it always returns a 0.

